<b>Interview 19: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;：, tour guide, female, 1980s, from Jiangsu</b> 

<p><b>What do you think of Kassel?</b>
<br>It's my first time out of the country, before, the furthest I travelled was to Anhui, in Zhejiang province. Before coming to Kassel, I always looked forward to seeing Europe's old castles as well as contemporary Western art, now that I'm here, many things have made a deep impression on me, for example the awareness of the people of Kassel, their love of art, and the way the sun doesn't set until 9:30 pm. China is a country with a large population, in Kassel I've really come to understand this differece, the number of people on the street here is nowhere equal to that on Huahai Road in Shanghai. Also, some say that because Documenta is happening, the population we see now isn't of this scale normally. 
That's crazy!

<p>"Fairytale" is really a very good project, we're still discussing wether we should give teacher &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; a flag, ha ha, I'm joking! It really feels like I'm living in a fairytale. Teacher &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; made dreams come true, he convinced people there can really still be fairytales in this world, that there are still things worth beliving in. This trip will change my life, by making me certain that miracles and fairytales can happen again. 

<p><b>What do think of the differences between Chinese and Western cultures?</b>
<br>From this experience, I've discovered a strange phenomenon: in this international art world Olympic Games-like grand occasion, there aren't many Chinese artists participating. Were they left behind, or do they basically have no opportunities to climb onto the world stage? Also, there are some works in the exhibition that I can't comprehend. This might be due to cultural differences. The West's audacity and passion created performance art that dominates worldwide, but Chinese-style art is silently doomed to exisit in but a small niche, unless it assimilates or completely changes. 

<p>In Germany, I've visited mostly churches. From this, I've seen that Europe's religious convicitions attach the most importance to the Christian God. I personnally think that they believe in God mostly for spiritual guidance, but most of us believe in religion for its implications on human nature, like Guanyin [Avalokitesvara Bodhisattva in Sanskrit, is a Goddess of Mercy in Buddhism] and "having many sons and daughters" and so on. 


<p><b>What do you think about love?</b>
<br>I really don't thing love is important, because in reality many people just get married to get married, they really aren't in love, they're just going to spend their lives together. I don't believe in love in modern society, love is as impossible as a fairytale. It's not always that when there's two people, one's love last longer, it doesn't matter if he's at home, if you can't stand his snoring, or worry about him cheating. In reality, love is hard to find, you have to bring together many of different elements, power, money, looks, reputation, there's also the issues of loyalty and dependence, after all, there are lots of beautiful people. But love really can boost artistic inspiration, [poet] Lu You's "Pink Soft Hands" [note: from the poem "Phoenix Hairpin"], [poet and calligrapher] Cao Zhi's "Godess of Luo" are both enduring poetic masterpieces. 

<p><b>How do you feel about your life?</b>
<br>My views on life are pessimistic, I don't know what I want, or why people are alive. I like freedom, therefore I don't like to work. I'm a slacker, I'm earnest and kindhearted, I have a wild imagination. For me, the most influential person or thing was a junior highschool classmate, one of our "Gang of Four", her grades weren't that good-- basically in China, if a girl's grades aren't good, their future is hopeless, unless she get married early, or leaves to go find odd jobs. She was adopted, her exam scores weren't good. She didn't study abroad, she wasn't held back, she didn't have the option of going to a vocational school, at 17 she started work in a factory, not long after she drank pesticide and kill herself. Since then, I keep on wondering, what do we live for? To carry on our ancestor's lineage, to keep this world going? Or just to eat, drink and be merry, as if we're just on earth by accident, for no reason? But as soon as you start thinking like this, you start to feel depraved, you don't want to go on, that's why even now, I still can't adopt the "great working people's"* proper attitude towards life or towards work. 

<p>
*Translator's note: the workers of Socialist theory, of the "glorious Chinese past"


